==English==

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|frm|médiocrité}}, from {{der|en|la|mediocritās}}, from {{m|la|mediocris}}.
{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₂eḱ-}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/miː.dɪˈɒk.ɹɪ.ti/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-mediocrity.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/mi.dɪˈɑk.ɹɪ.ti/|[mi.dɪˈɑk.ɹɪ.ɾi]}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|now|_|rare}} The quality of being [[intermediate]] between two extremes; a [[mean]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A middle course of action; [[moderation]], [[balance]].
#* {{RQ:Burton Melancholy|edition=2nd}}, New York Review Books 2001, p.273:
#*: In adversity I wish for prosperity, and in prosperity I am afraid of adversity. What '''mediocrity''' may be found?
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The condition of being [[mediocre]]; having only an average degree of quality, skills etc.; no better than standard.
#* {{quote-web|en|date=28 March 2021|author=Phil McNulty|title=Albania 0-2 England|work=BBC Sport|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/56472933|passage=England captain {{w|Harry Kane}} lifted the '''mediocrity''' of an attritional first half on a slow surface when he scored his 33rd goal for his country, a superbly guided diving header from {{w|Luke Shaw}}'s cross seven minutes before the interval.}}
# An individual with mediocre abilities or achievements.

